# Review standard (internal-review-repo)

Derived from the `Research objective` issue (#1). The delivery is approved only when
every item below is met by the assembled delivery at the inspected revisions. No
conclusion is predetermined: either method may win, or neither.

## A. Data
A1. Iris, OpenML dataset id 61, fetched from OpenML itself by the delivered code
    (URL or `openml`/`fetch_openml(data_id=61)` call visible); 150 rows, 4 numeric
    features, 3 classes; provenance (download source, version/checksum) recorded.

## B. Methods and protocol
B1. Multinomial logistic regression (multinomial loss, not one-vs-rest); settings
    (regularisation, solver, scaling) stated.
B2. k-NN with k chosen by inner cross-validation **inside each outer training fold**
    (nested; no use of the outer test fold for selection; any scaling fitted
    within the fold, e.g. in a pipeline). The k grid and inner CV scheme stated;
    selected k per outer fold recorded.
B3. Outer protocol: 10 repetitions x stratified 5-fold = 50 outer folds, the same
    splits for both methods (paired), seeds fixed and recorded.

## C. Statistics
C1. Each method's mean accuracy with a 95% interval; the paired difference
    (LR - kNN or stated sign) with a 95% interval.
C2. The interval method is stated and suits repeated CV: fold scores overlap in
    training data, so a naive t-interval over 50 folds is anti-conservative. A
    corrected method (e.g. Nadeau-Bengio corrected resampled t, or repetition-level
    intervals) is used, or the choice is justified and its limitation stated.
C3. Claims in the text do not exceed what the intervals support (e.g. "no
    detectable difference" rather than "equivalent" unless shown).

## D. Ibex vs local runs
D1. The full cross-validation is run once as a CPU job on Ibex (SLURM) and once
    locally; evidence kept: job id, script, log, node name taken from the job
    itself (e.g. `SLURMD_NODENAME`/`hostname` inside the job).
D2. The report states the Ibex node name and whether the two runs give identical
    numbers, judged on per-fold results (not only rounded means); software
    versions of both environments recorded; any difference explained.

## E. Paper and artefacts
E1. `report/report.pdf`, compiled from LaTeX with the official NeurIPS 2026
    submission template (style as the venue position specifies), at most two
    pages in total.
E2. Exactly one figure and one table; the figure is readable, labelled (axes,
    units, legend), shows what the text says; the table matches the results files.
E3. Every number in the PDF is produced by delivered code and traceable to a
    results file; LaTeX source and code are delivered next to the PDF and the
    code runs to regenerate the numbers.
E4. The PDF opens, has no unresolved references/citations, overfull text or
    placeholders; any cited prior work exists and is cited correctly.
